{"title":"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 100","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_100&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> be a root of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/1/3316d5198158e6bda4ea2aa37d064bb3b4f7c3e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^6-x-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"80\" height=\"15\" />, and call two polynomials <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> with integer coefficients <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/8/ae8f2c2d2450c3498877bbc2c5bd08dd8e7b05a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{equivalent}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"17\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/9/4490a141ea33bdb7f165861e7ba9245938e44f0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p(\\alpha)\\equiv q(\\alpha)\\pmod3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"165\" height=\"18\" />. It is known that every such polynomial is equivalent to exactly one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/9/51936da459b1cb5af9d836e4b29b7ff6809f8116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0,1,x,x^2,\\ldots,x^{727}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"141\" height=\"18\" />. Find the largest integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/0/fc052b8d23e4a0031d669bdf8f1d5882b5977b07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&lt;728$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> for which there exists a polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/2/ab2a5d1a13bd8a497eed9c353dfd84e6e336dddb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^3-p-x^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"20\" /> is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_100&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/d/77dd9cdeb7af426fa03ca853cf8c38ecfcb53e95.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Solution}\\]\" width=\"68\" height=\"11\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/4/bb4ed1ebb8d092aa32df873f9ac3713810316379.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{We work in the ring } \\mathbb{F}_3[\\alpha], \\text{ where } \\alpha^6 = \\alpha + 1 \\text{ (mod }3\\text{), since } \\alpha \\text{ is a root of } x^6 - x - 1.\\]\" width=\"618\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897c0aa1fbff49c416a1e4e4d29b758a09fe87a6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Thus every polynomial reduces to a linear combination of } 1,\\alpha,\\alpha^2,\\ldots,\\alpha^5.\\]\" width=\"543\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/d/07d87601c4f6e4e55483a12cb165806b25aa5a4e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{We are told there are } 728 = 3^6 - 1 \\text{ nonzero equivalence classes, so } \\mathbb{F}*3(\\alpha) \\text{ is the finite field } \\mathbb{F}*{3^6}.\\]\" width=\"709\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/addbaa4ddd04b4d36697f91b9bc3992da01b92f6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Hence } \\alpha \\text{ generates the multiplicative group } \\mathbb{F}_{3^6}^\\times, \\text{ which has order } 3^6 - 1 = 728.\\]\" width=\"573\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/6/af6a497f108b60dedfdb4084caac0a2456aa754e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Step 1: Reformulating the condition}\\]\" width=\"303\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448102a628028c391531d66985e769e8c74a50a7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p^3 - p \\equiv \\alpha^n \\pmod{3}.\\]\" width=\"179\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/a/59adad29e3d537cbcc658dbdb7bd5efc3e358671.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Define } f(x) = x^3 - x. \\text{ Over } \\mathbb{F}_{3^6}, \\text{ this map is } \\mathbb{F}_3\\text{-linear.}\\]\" width=\"404\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da270e756f9d9cdae4435427318fef195ddb348b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Its kernel is } {x \\in \\mathbb{F}_{3^6} : x^3 = x} = \\mathbb{F}_3,\\]\" width=\"262\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/8/6c8fde1262b788e401456628145508018f3dcd8b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{so } |\\ker f| = 3.\\]\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82b0dbe8122f2377b5f1b26eead6eff6509f3a39.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Thus } |\\text{Im}(f)| = \\frac{3^6}{3} = 3^5 = 243.\\]\" width=\"236\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/7/f87f244dbff6075d5af0375cc809877845cf347d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Step 2: Characterizing the image}\\]\" width=\"277\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d15faf735e1040b6cac2a7e2839bbb866194a5d4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{A standard fact in finite fields: } x^3 - x \\text{ maps onto the set of elements with trace } 0.\\]\" width=\"592\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594f2dca493edf02cd2cb3034587dcbba875b2af.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Thus } \\alpha^n \\text{ lies in the image iff } \\mathrm{Tr}(\\alpha^n) = 0,\\]\" width=\"303\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/1030cd750ab4bba4ddc4f7b23429a24dc849bb57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{where } \\mathrm{Tr}(x) = x + x^3 + x^{3^2} + x^{3^3} + x^{3^4} + x^{3^5}.\\]\" width=\"355\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/7/8a7e54b99d1d2682552c62dfd318ae889f4e0ecb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Step 3: Apply to } \\alpha^n\\]\" width=\"166\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/2/582b8ff0d37cc6d3265003390438242c118a9d2a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathrm{Tr}(\\alpha^n) = \\alpha^n + \\alpha^{3n} + \\alpha^{3^2 n} + \\alpha^{3^3 n} + \\alpha^{3^4 n} + \\alpha^{3^5 n}.\\]\" width=\"375\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/4/e34323ebe872754bd6f6682c6808715adfa836ac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{This is a sum over the Frobenius orbit of } \\alpha^n.\\]\" width=\"327\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/5/c45bf225c08c57a9ecc1388419b350529b29cf37.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Step 4: When is the trace zero?}\\]\" width=\"265\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73f137ca596db0ca73165b06bd3d73607455d1e4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{In } \\mathbb{F}_{3^6}^\\times, \\text{ conjugates correspond to exponents modulo } 728.\\]\" width=\"414\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/8/bc880e0224bbe8b43eff1f4dc864efe882ff5702.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Thus we look for the largest } n &lt; 728 \\text{ such that}\\]\" width=\"344\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/3/533388f118a3310c9f13e0ac54b351835c4d9fa3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\alpha^n + \\alpha^{3n} + \\alpha^{9n} + \\alpha^{27n} + \\alpha^{81n} + \\alpha^{243n} = 0.\\]\" width=\"331\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e81ef5be3739791f49ef905fa652e5fe3b0d4034.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Key idea: } \\text{This holds exactly when the orbit sum vanishes, i.e., when } n \\text{ is not fixed by any smaller cycle.}\\]\" width=\"772\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/e/61e8561fc9207129569df90a581fcf3500c78db2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{The largest such } n \\text{ corresponds to the largest exponent whose orbit has full size } 6.\\]\" width=\"598\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/2/7627d35b054282354ac84fe234afbe8a72dbabb2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Since } 728 = 2^3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 13, \\text{ the Frobenius action has order } 6.\\]\" width=\"418\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/7/557e49c23efaabaaecca6de63e26bc68dd58c744.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{The largest } n &lt; 728 \\text{ with full orbit (i.e., not stabilized early) is } 727.\\]\" width=\"498\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a5cd90d10c22a4bcf770beb8da27b8556f32333.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{Final Answer: } \\boxed{727}\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=~Trashboycan\" title=\"~Trashboycan\">~Trashboycan</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017215900\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.017 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.027 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 141/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8185/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:16657:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017215900 and revision id 280594. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}